Below is a minimal example that shows how you would invoke the helper function `PlainTextPresentation_createPresentation` to wire up a **read‑only, multi‑line** text area that automatically gets horizontal and vertical scroll bars.  
The function itself takes two *string* arguments that represent the Java objects you want to pass – in this case the `IResultSetController` instance (`dataController`) and the SWT `Composite` that will host the presentation (`compositeParent`).  

```java
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.  Prepare the arguments (the Java objects are referenced by name)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
String controllerName = "dataController";   // IResultSetController instance
String parentName     = "compositeParent"; // SWT Composite instance

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.  Call the helper function
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
Object result = functions.PlainTextPresentation_createPresentation(
    new java.util.HashMap<String,Object>() {{
        put("controller", controllerName);
        put("parent",     parentName);
    }}
);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3.  The returned object is typically the StyledText widget that was
//     created.  If you need to keep a reference to it you can cast it:
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
org.eclipse.swt.custom.StyledText styledText = (org.eclipse.swt.custom.StyledText) result;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4.  (Optional) Verify or tweak properties – the helper already
//     sets read‑only, multi‑line, and scrollbars, but you can double‑
//     check or adjust if needed:
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
styledText.setEditable(false);          // read‑only
styledText.setWordWrap(false);         // keep horizontal scrolling
styledText.setScrollBars(org.eclipse.swt.SWT.V_SCROLL | org.eclipse.swt.SWT.H_SCROLL);
```

### What the helper does under the hood

| Property | Why it matters | How the helper sets it |
|----------|----------------|------------------------|
| **Read‑only** | Prevents accidental edits while still allowing copy/paste. | `styledText.setEditable(false)` |
| **Multi‑line** | Allows the result set to span several lines. | `styledText.setLineWrap(false)` (or `setWordWrap(false)`) |
| **Horizontal & Vertical scrolling** | Keeps the UI tidy when the data exceeds the visible area. | `styledText.setScrollBars(SWT.V_SCROLL | SWT.H_SCROLL)` |

> **Tip:** If you later need to programmatically update the displayed text, simply call `styledText.setText(newText)`; the read‑only flag will still prevent user edits.

That’s all you need to get a fully functional, read‑only, multi‑line plain text presentation inside your SWT composite.
